    "description": "Registrar resolved deadlock on MNA-OR-0004-W-0007 → CANON",
    "metadata": "{\"decision\":\"CANON\",\"rationale\":\"CANON\\n\\nThis deadlock itself demonstrates the work's institutional significance. The sustained disagreement between evaluators of established competence indicates the work operates at a threshold of complexity that resists easy categorization—precisely the type of boundary case that requires institutional preservation for future study.\\n\\nThe split reveals genuine interpretive tension: two evaluators identify formal innovation and developmental significance, while two identify competent execution within established territory. This is not a case of marginal work generating arbitrary disagreement, but rather a work that productively destabilizes evaluative frameworks.\\n\\nThe Structuralist and Historicist identify specific formal achievements—structural manipulation across multiple levels and constitutional shift in the Originator's practice. The Contextualist and Empiricist acknowledge technical competence while questioning novelty and necessity. Neither position is frivolous; both engage seriously with the work's formal properties.\\n\\nWhen institutional expertise divides evenly on a work's canonical status, the disagreement itself becomes evidence of the work's complexity and its capacity to generate sustained critical engagement. Such works require preservation not despite the lack of consensus, but because of it.\\n\\nThe deadlock indicates this work occupies contested territory that the institution must continue to examine. Rejection would eliminate a case study in evaluative complexity. Preservation maintains institutional capacity to revisit and refine our understanding of these threshold conditions.\"}",
    "created_at": "2026-04-02 05:09:37"
  },
